						<string>Melt the butter in a large broad pan over medium heat. Add the diced onion, salt, and a modest shake of Montreal seasoning. Cook patiently until completely soft, translucent, and sweet, then continue until golden-brown edges and occasional crispy bits appear. Taste an onyo. If the response is “yes, very yes,” proceed.</string>
					</dict>
					<dict>
						<key>id</key>
						<string>E3D80002-0000-4000-8000-000000000002</string>
						<key>name</key>
						<string>Deploy SOSIG</string>
						<key>text</key>
						<string>Add the sliced sausage. Spread everything out as much as practical and leave it alone for stretches so some sausage faces brown properly. Stir and redistribute occasionally, building rendered fat and fond without sacrificing the onions.</string>
					</dict>
					<dict>
						<key>id</key>
						<string>E3D80003-0000-4000-8000-000000000003</string>
						<key>name</key>
						<string>Cook the tomato paste hard</string>
						<key>text</key>
						<string>Work the tomato paste thoroughly into the fat and cook it from bright red toward deep brick-red or orange. Let some contact the pan directly. Add the small glug of vodka, scrape up the fond, and cook until the obvious alcohol aroma has largely disappeared.</string>
					</dict>
				</array>
				<key>title</key>
				<string>Build the foundation</string>
			</dict>
			<dict>
				<key>id</key>
				<string>E3D70002-0000-4000-8000-000000000002</string>
				<key>steps</key>
				<array>
					<dict>
						<key>id</key>
						<string>E3D80004-0000-4000-8000-000000000004</string>
						<key>name</key>
						<string>Assemble the sauce</string>
						<key>text</key>
						<string>Add the Ro-Tel, liquid and all, and scrape the pan clean. Cook it into the concentrated tomato mixture for a few minutes. Add the garlic, both cans of prepared Hunt's meat pasta sauce, bay leaf, oregano, basil, red pepper flakes, long pepper, and the tiniest imaginable pinch of clove. Bring to a gentle simmer.</string>
					</dict>
					<dict>
						<key>duration</key>
						<dict>
							<key>seconds</key>
							<integer>1800</integer>
						</dict>
						<key>id</key>
						<string>E3D80005-0000-4000-8000-000000000005</string>
						<key>name</key>
						<string>Stop screwing with it</string>
						<key>text</key>
						<string>Leave the spice cabinet alone and simmer gently for at least 30 minutes, preferably longer, stirring and scraping periodically. Taste. If it is balanced but still tomato-bright, add about 1 teaspoon sugar to the entire batch. Near the end, add a few drops of Worcestershire for invisible savory depth. Adjust only what the sauce actually asks for, then remove the bay leaf.</string>
					</dict>
